\begin{abstract}
In federated learning (FL), the accuracy and convergence speed of the system are greatly influenced by data heterogeneity and the weights shared during the training. To reduce the effect of data and weights divergence, the use of coalitions, groups of devices or agents inside a network that have priority for sharing the weights, has a significant positive impact. In this work, we compare the effects and dynamics that various similarity measures have on the coalitions formation and evolution of a decentralized FL system.

\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
federated learning, coalitions, similarity
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
The rise of Federated Learning (FL) techniques has improved the way that distributed devices can train neural networks, allowing true DFL (Decentralized Federated Learning) without the need to centralize the data. This comes at the cost of performance on traditional FL, due to the presence of non-IID (Independent and Identically Distributed) data across participating agents \cite{b1}. The lack of a central agent that controls the data and the weights of the agents, this data heterogeneity causes a great hit on the performance thanks to weight divergence.

To solve this problems the use of \textit{coalitions} has gained attention. A coalition is a group of agents that prioritize sharing model weights between themselves than with others, limiting the weight mixing with others neighbors. This makes the coalitions learn and start converging towards the same path \cite{b2}.

One usually overlook aspect when discussing about dynamic coalitions in federated learning, is the criterion to create and manage the coalitions once the system already started. In this work we explore the effects of using different measures of similarity for that purpose.

\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Consensus-based Learning algorithms}

Consensus, in the context of FL, is an iterative and distributed algorithm, whose goal is to make all agents agree on the same weights. To reach this goal, the algorithm makes use of communication interactions and the rules that govern the MAS (Multi-Agent System).

For machine learning, the consensus algorithm most commonly used is \textbf{FedAvg} (Federated Averaging) \cite{b9}, this algorithm consist in apply the average between the weights of two models trained in parallel. This approach is the most simple and flawed because it does not protect the model from non-IID (Independent and identically distributed) data that mess up their gradients \cite{b1}.

Other variations FedAvg have mechanisms to combat a sudden change of gradients, but require a central node that tries to minimize the drift of gradients and correct the weights, such as Federated Proximal (FedProx), introduces a measure of proximity to calculate the weight of each agent on the consensus \cite{b11}; or Federated Averaging with Server Momentum (FedAvgM) \cite{b12} .

DFML (Decentralized Federated Mutual Learning) \cite{b10}, is a version of decentralized FedAvg where an agent takes the temporal role of weight aggregator, allowing to apply mechanisms of protection for non-IID in a decentralized context.

\subsection{Coalitions in agent-based Decentralized Federated Learning}

A coalition is a structured subset of agents that agree to coordinate their behavior toward a shared objective while preserving their individual autonomy. In MAS, coalitions act as an organizational layer between fully independent agents. Each agent remains an independent decision-making entity but adopts additional interaction rules within the coalition, such as preferential communication, information sharing, or joint decision protocols.

In the context of DFL, a coalition defines the \textbf{set of peers} with which an agent exchanges model parameters, gradients, or similarity information. The coalition therefore restricts the effective communication graph and shapes how knowledge propagates through the system. Rather than interacting with the entire population, an agent prioritizes collaboration with coalition members that satisfy a given compatibility criterion. This criterion can be based on properties of the agents’ local data, their position in the network topology, or their observed learning behavior.

Coalitions introduce a mechanism to \textbf{manage heterogeneity}. When agents operate on non-IID data or under different environmental constraints, unrestricted model mixing can degrade convergence. Coalition formation allows agents with compatible characteristics to intensify cooperation while limiting interference from incompatible peers. At the same time, coalitions do not imply isolation: inter-coalition communication can still occur with a different probability, which preserves global information flow.


\subsection{Taxonomy of Coalitions}

A useful way to position coalition strategies is to describe them along three orthogonal axes.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Coalition formation criterion}: semantical vs positional.
    A first distinction is the criterion used to decide whether two agents should belong to the same coalition. Surveys of FL coalitions separate (i) semantic formation, where agents are grouped because their local data (or learning task) shares meaning or structure, from (ii) positional formation, where grouping depends on geographical proximity and communication constraints (range, neighborhood in a communication graph). 
    This distinction also appears in coalition-enabled consensus learning proposals, where coalitions are motivated either by proximity in space (to reduce communication cost) or proximity in the meaning of tasks and data (to reduce harmful mixing under heterogeneity). 
    
    \item \textbf{Persistence of membership}: static vs dynamic.
    Both semantic and positional coalitions can be static or dynamic. In static formation, the coalition assignment is fixed after initialization; agents maintain the same coalition membership throughout. 
    In dynamic formation, membership can change during execution, including join/leave events and reassignment driven by context (data drift, mobility, availability). 
    A concrete positional example is WANET-style settings in which each agent’s neighborhood changes over time due to mobility and the antenna's limited range; coalitions emerge from the set of peers reachable at each time step. 

    \item \textbf{Semantic method of creation}: manual vs automatic.
    Semantic coalition formation groups agents according to the meaning and structure of their local data. In this setting, coalition membership depends on a representation of semantics that can be obtained through manual expert design or through automatic data-driven mechanisms. In both cases, coalitions can be configured as static or dynamic structures.
    
    \textit{Manual semantic formation} relies on human expert knowledge to define the conceptual framework that agents use to assess similarity. A domain expert participates in the design of ontologies, taxonomies, or metadata schemas that encode relationships between concepts relevant to the learning task. Agents use these shared structures to interpret their local data and to determine coalition membership. The semantic space is therefore grounded in externally curated knowledge rather than inferred only from observations. Manual semantic coalitions can be static, when ontology-based assignments remain fixed during training, or dynamic, when agents update their coalition based on rule-driven reinterpretation of expert-defined categories as new information becomes available.
    
    \textit{Automatic semantic formation} derives coalition structure directly from data without requiring human curation of the semantic model. Agents compute similarity using learned representations such as embeddings, latent features, or clustering outputs that approximate semantic proximity. The coalition structure emerges from algorithmic comparison of these representations rather than from predefined expert rules. Automatic semantic coalitions can also be static or dynamic. In the static case, the grouping is computed once from an initial representation and remains unchanged. In the dynamic case, agents periodically recompute representations and adjust membership to reflect data drift, model evolution, or changing task similarity.
    
    Manual approaches emphasize interpretability and alignment with domain concepts, while automatic approaches emphasize adaptability and scalability. Both variants support static and dynamic realizations, which determine whether coalition boundaries are treated as fixed design choices or as evolving properties of the learning process.

\end{enumerate}


\section{Methodology}


\subsection{Similarity Measures}
    In the context of comparing similarity measures, it is important to remark that similarity, difference, and distance measures, their name only indicates if they maximize the difference (e.g., As more distance there is between two points, more different they are) or if the maximize the similarity (e.g., As more aligned the direction of two vectors are, the more similar they are). As seen in Table \ref{tab:measuresPropertiesList}, the selection of measures to compare each belongs to a different category.

    None of the similarity measures chosen to be compared can be applied directly over the weights of neural networks; as such, there is need to normalize the weights before comparison. There are multiple ways of apply the normalization, with different problems. The one we apply on this work is getting a single vector from all the trainable parameters of a network, this aproach has the potential problem of treating every change of the same magnitude in the vector with the same relevance, when in reality the weights of the output layer are more relevant over all for the output of the network\cite{b3}.
    
    The other potential problem with comparing the similarity in this way, is that this methodology can't detect structural differences or even the exact same weights changed their position during training\cite{b4}. This isn't the case in the context of federated learning, given the common initial weight start and the constant apply of consensus, limits greatly the Loss Landscape and the convergence of the model. This last part is only true if the consensus is frequent enough.

    The similarity measures chosen for the study are the following:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Euclidean Distance} \cite{b13}: Measures the straight-line distance between two points in multi-dimensional space. Can be used to measure the magnitude difference between vectors.
        \item \textbf{Cosine Similarity} \cite{b13}: Measures the orientation between two vectors, regardless of their magnitude.
        \item \textbf{Jaccard Index} \cite{b13}: Measures the structural similarity between sample sets by calculating the ratio of overlapping close values.
        \item \textbf{Mean Squared Error} \cite{b14}: Measures the average squared intensity of error between predicted and actual values by averaging the squares of the differences.
        \item \textbf{Cross-Entropy Loss}: \cite{b15} Measures the divergence between two probability distributions.
    \end{itemize}
    
    
