\subsection{Consensus Algorithm}
In this work, the focus of the study is on the effects in decentralized networks, and as such we need to use Gossip Averaging \cite{b8} for the lack of a central node that organizes and changes the weights of the agents. 

The concrete algorithm used is a base version of FedAvg and mixes the weights as shown in \textbf{Algorithm \ref{alg:consensus}}.

\begin{algorithm}[]
\label{alg:consensus}
\caption{FedAvg and Similarity Tracking.}
\SetKwFunction{FCheck}{applyWeightConsensus}
\SetKwInOut{Input}{Entrada}
\SetKwInOut{Output}{Resultado}

\FCheck{}\;
    \BlankLine
    \While{$\text{weightReceivedList} \neq \emptyset$}{
        % Extraer el primer elemento del buffer
        $(n, w_{neigh}) \gets \text{weightReceivedList.pop}(0)$\;
        
        \BlankLine
        $\text{sim} \gets \text{rateSimilarity}(w_{neigh}, \theta)$\;
        $\text{neighbor\_info}[n] \gets \text{sim}$\;
        
        \BlankLine
        $\epsilon \gets \frac{1}{|\text{neighborhood}|}$\;
    
        $\theta \gets \theta + \epsilon \cdot (w_{neigh} - \theta)$\;
    }
\end{algorithm}
This simpler approach allows that during the evaluation of the experiments most of the variations are caused exclusively by the similarity measures, other consensus algorithm variation like \textbf{DFML} would obfuscate the origin of the differences in the results.

\subsection{Coalition Algorithm}

The focus of this work is the interaction between different similarity measures and the coalitions formed in a decentralized federated learning framework, this means that there is no central point controlling what agent forms part of which coalition.

For this work we use a self appointment coalition algorithm in which every agent has it's own interpretation of what agents form part of their coalition. In this algorithm \ref{alg:coalition} having precalculated the similarity with the neighbors and starting with an initial coalition formed by all neighbor agents.

We use a variable called \textbf{threshold\_similarity} to determine the flexibility of the coalition, a threshold similarity of 1 means that every time the coalition updates would reduce its members until there is only 1 member, besides the own agent. On the other side, threshold similarity doesn't have a maximum value but a too big of a value would impede the exclusion of agents diverging, values between 1.48 and 1.6, normally rounded to 1.5 are commonly used and prove effective \cite{b5}.

\begin{algorithm}[]
\label{alg:coalition}
\caption{Dynamic update of the coalition based on similarity thresholds.}
\SetKwFunction{FCheck}{UpdateCoalition}
\SetKwInOut{Input}{Entrada}
\SetKwInOut{Output}{Resultado}

\FCheck{}\;

    \BlankLine
    
    $\mathcal{C} \gets \{ \text{name} \mid \text{name} \in \text{coalitionNeighbors} \}$\;
    
    $\text{avgSim} \gets \frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}|} \sum_{n \in \mathcal{C}} \text{neighbor\_info}[n]$\;
    \BlankLine

    $\text{new\_coalition} \gets \emptyset$\;
    \ForEach{$(\text{name, similarity}) \in \text{neighbor\_info}$}{
        \eIf{\text{IS\_SIMILARITY}}{
            \If{$\text{similarity} > \frac{\text{avgSim}}{\text{threshold\_similarity}}$}{
                $\text{new\_coalition} \gets \text{new\_coalition} \cup \{\text{name}\}$\;
            }
        }{
            \If{$\text{similarity} < \text{avgSim} \times \text{threshold\_similarity}$}{
                $\text{new\_coalition} \gets \text{new\_coalition} \cup \{\text{name}\}$\;
            }
        }
    }
    \Return $\text{new\_coalition}$\;
\end{algorithm}

