\subsection{Experiments}

To realize the comparative we decided to simulate the process of training neural networks in a large amount of agents using a simple Stochastic Gradient Descent\cite{b6} on a multidimensional vector, and use it like the weights of the neural networks.

The important static parameters that will stay fixed during the different experiments, and some are the cause of some of the quirks of the training, are:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Number of agents}: 1000, making it a graph with 1000 different unique nodes.

\item \textbf{Grade of the graph}: 8, this makes that not only every agent only has 8 unique neighbor agents, but that the maximum size of every coalition is 8, plus the node of the agent that calculates it.

\item \textbf{Dimensionality of the weights}: 100, the size of this vector was decided after testing, projecting the evolution of the simulated training, increasing the size until showed the properties of real networks and don't converging linearly in an instant.


\item \textbf{Learning rate}: 0.01, average value for learning rate.
\item \textbf{Scale of Noise}: 0.5, a value that determines how much noise is introduced turing the gradient descent to ensure it doesn't become a straight line, the value is slightly increased above the average.

\item \textbf{Number of epochs}: 100, it would be a low number if it were the training of a real neural network but it's more than enough number of iterations to run these experiments.
\end{itemize}

One of the quirks on the training that these parameters cause is the low grade on a big graph, that could cause that multiple coalitions converge in close values while the coalitions themselves are more disperse.

During the experiments on the selected similarity measures we will measure 6 different metrics to analyze the state of the system after training and how the coalitions worked and evolved:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Average Final Coalition Size}: Average size of the coalition of every agent, 1 would be the minimum and 8 the maximum in with the parameters.

\item \textbf{Average Intra-Coalition Distance}: Average of the average euclidean distance that the agent has with the members of it's coalition.

\item \textbf{Average Coalition Divergence}: Average of the squared distances between the agents of the same coalition, divided by the number of members of the coalition. This measure is more precise than the Average Intra-Coalition distance because the square makes more clear when the weights are more disperse.

\item \textbf{Average Converged Coalition distance}: Average distance between the average weight of the coalitions.

\item \textbf{Average Final Error}: Average final error of all agents at the end of the training.

\item \textbf{Reciprocity Percentage}: Percentage of how many agents are part of the coalitions of the agents they have in their coalition.
\end{itemize}

Besides these values, we also experiment with different values for threshold similarity, 1.75, 1.50 and 1.25, being 1 the minimum possible and 2 would cause the coalitions to be too flexible to the changes of weights; That makes these 3 values the more interesting to test, being 1.50 the one referenced on literature \cite{b5}.

