\subsection{Exposition of the results}

The different experiments are using the same parameters during their execution, for a better comparative, as expressed on tables \ref{tab:elasticityhigh}, \ref{tab:elasticitymid} and \ref{tab:elasticitylow}.

An important point to clarify before doing the analysis of the results, is that all experiments converged correctly in every instance, due to the way the experiment was conducted, with a simplistic version of gradient descent, this more simple approach guaranteed that all the agents on the network converged in a similar way, as we can see in the figure \ref{fig:evolutionPCAcos} and \ref{fig:evolutionPCAeuc}.


\begin{figure}[htbp]
\label{fig:evolutionPCAeuc}
\caption{Example of the evolution of a system with 10 agents.}
\centering
    \centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{10_agents_euclidean.png}}
\centering
\end{figure}

In the case of the table \ref{tab:elasticityhigh}, we can observe a great reciprocity percentage, that and the fact that for an 8 grade graph the average coalition size, which excludes the own agent, is in all cases superior to 7 means that means that there are few cases where an agent has been excluded from a coalition, the average coalition Divergence proves that in all five measures tested. The outlier in this case is Jaccard Index, with a 100\si{\percent} of reciprocity index, and an average of 8 members on the coalition out of 8.

From the results of table \ref{tab:elasticitymid}, it's clear that the most affected measures for the change of the threshold similarity are the euclidean distance and MSE, and they, thanks to the similar formula, show closer results. The measures of Jaccard and Cross-Entropy Loss, show over all the same results as in the previous case, meaning that the variations on the average similarity between agents weren't affected as much with the threshold. The case of cosine similarity is more interesting, because it changed it's values the same way Euclidean Distance and MSE did, but in a lower scale.

In the case of table \ref{tab:elasticitylow}, the threshold similarity affected all the measures, except cross-entropy loss, that changes some values slightly showing the change had a real effect on it. Meanwhile Jaccard Index has some changes on the reciprocity percentage, but minimal in other values. Euclidean distance and MSE continue their tendency of behaving in similar way. Cosine similarity seems to be affected at much slower rate by the threshold similarity but clearly it affects it in the same way, that does to euclidean distance and MSE.

\subsection{Interpretation of the results}
As mentioned before Jaccard Index an in lower way Cross-Entropy Loss, work as similarity measures of other domains, Jaccard can work to measure structural differences, when training different networks with the same architecture, but in the context of federated learning with consensus algorithm, unless the value of threshold similarity is close to 1, it will return high values of similarity all the time. The same can be said with cross-entropy loss, with he difference that is less consistent in the ratings for being a probabilistic distance measure.

Euclidean distance and MSE, work treating the weights like a point in the space and calculating the magnitude of the difference between them in a line, that makes them more sensible to the changes to threshold similarity, that sensibility also makes them in measures that can be easely to control with a parameter.

Cosine similarity treats the weights like vectors and return the alineation between them, that makes it so it can be more sensible to structural differences than euclidean distance or MSE could be, but in the context of federated learning the structures learned for the networks will be almost identical, it is still affected by the changes on threshold similarity, but with a lower rate because it's a similarity measure and also has less consistency because it is a angular similarity measure, that means that if two weights are aligned, their similarity will be higher even if the magnitude difference is the higher between their neighbors.

